# Java Notes: Exception and IllegalThreadStateException

## 1. Exception
An exception is an event that disrupts the normal flow of a program during execution.

Example: Dividing an integer by zero causes `ArithmeticException`.

## 2. IllegalThreadStateException
It is an unchecked exception that occurs when an operation is performed on a thread in an invalid state.

Example: Starting the same thread more than once.

## 3. Thread
A thread is a small unit of execution within a program.

## 4. start() Method
The `start()` method starts a new thread and allows it to execute its task.

Important: A thread object can be started only once.

## 5. Example Program

    class IllegalThreadStateDemo {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
                System.out.println("Thread is running");
            });

            t.start();
            t.start(); // Throws IllegalThreadStateException
        }
    }

## 6. Output

    Thread is running
    Exception in thread "main" java.lang.IllegalThreadStateException

Note: The order of the output lines may vary.

## 7. Important Points
- An exception disrupts normal program execution.
- `IllegalThreadStateException` is an unchecked exception.
- The `start()` method can be called only once on a thread object.
- Calling `start()` again on the same thread object throws `IllegalThreadStateException`.
- `try-catch` can be used to handle exceptions.

## Memory Trick
One thread object = One start() call.
